WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:07.410
So once again, we have a claim that there
are Republican senators willing, ready or

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:12.210
interested in convicting Donald Trump in the
expected impeachment trial in the United States

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:13.210
Senate.

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:19.570
Now, we had a story a week or two ago, uh,
that, uh, had a democratic Senator Chris Murphy

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:22.199
saying that there are Republican senators.

00:00:22.199 --> 00:00:26.619
He says you can count them on one hand, but
that there are Republican senators who are,

00:00:26.619 --> 00:00:30.679
uh, ready to convict Donald Trump in the Senate.

00:00:30.679 --> 00:00:32.070
There was really no evidence of this.

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:35.150
It was just Chris Murphy saying that that's
what he believes.

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:38.880
We have another person saying this, but this
time it is a Republican, not a Democrat.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:44.660
It's bill weld, former Republican governor
of Massachusetts who is also running, I guess

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:46.840
in some technical sense, although it's not
going anywhere.

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.290
A primary campaign against Donald Trump.

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:53.450
Bill weld was interviewed by the Hill and
he said, quote, I know most of the senior

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:57.190
Republicans in the Senate, they're picking
their words carefully when they talk to me

00:00:57.190 --> 00:01:01.370
of all people, even though we are friends,
I wouldn't want to get quoted.

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:06.289
I don't even like to ask someone to do something
which is not in their political self-interest.

00:01:06.289 --> 00:01:12.249
But yeah, I would say they're four to six
votes for removal right now.

00:01:12.249 --> 00:01:14.420
So there are two responses to this.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:17.750
Number one is no way, and number two is, so
what?

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:19.789
And I'll explain each of those responses.

00:01:19.789 --> 00:01:23.631
First of all, there's no evidence right now
that there are really four to six Republican

00:01:23.631 --> 00:01:27.399
senators ready to convict Donald Trump on
impeachment in the Senate.

00:01:27.399 --> 00:01:32.409
Every indication we have so far is that they
will go against impeachment Republicans in

00:01:32.409 --> 00:01:33.409
the Senate.

00:01:33.409 --> 00:01:38.679
Will, you might even see a centrist Democrat
like Joe mansion also go against impeachment

00:01:38.679 --> 00:01:39.810
in the Senate.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:44.540
But can we come up with a list that is at
least circumstantially reasonable of Republicans

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:46.679
that might vote to convict Donald Trump?

00:01:46.679 --> 00:01:47.679
Sure.

00:01:47.679 --> 00:01:48.679
We talked about it last week.

00:01:48.679 --> 00:01:52.729
Susan Collins in Maine, often a candidate
has been an occasional critic of Trump, but

00:01:52.729 --> 00:01:57.329
the caveat is in the end she'll say a lot
of things, but in the end she'll get in line

00:01:57.329 --> 00:02:01.659
like she did with Brett Kavanaugh and his
confirmation to the Supreme court.

00:02:01.659 --> 00:02:03.280
Mitt Romney, Senator from Utah.

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:05.779
Mitt Romney has had many public disagreements
with Donald Trump.

00:02:05.779 --> 00:02:10.130
They've had very acute, uh, um, uh, bad blood
between them.

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:12.190
I guess I would say it's conceivable.

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:16.750
Mitt Romney might say he would impeach, but
previously when asked, he has not given any

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:21.319
indication that he would Lisa Murkowski from
Alaska, her name always comes up anytime they

00:02:21.319 --> 00:02:26.200
say, well, here's a more reasonable Republican
who might depart from Republican orthodoxy.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.320
Her name is mentioned Cory Gardner.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:29.320
There's a couple of others.

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:34.620
But as I said before, this is where we go
from no way to, so what, even in four to six

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:36.330
Republicans vote to convict Trump.

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:41.799
It won't remove Trump and it will get those
Republicans ostracized by the Republican party.

00:02:41.799 --> 00:02:47.680
Because remember, you need two thirds of the
Senate, that's 67 senators to vote to convict.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:53.439
In order to remove the president, there are
45 Democrats and to independence that caucus

00:02:53.439 --> 00:02:58.010
with the Democrats, that means you need to
find 20 Republicans.

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:01.310
If you have maybe four to six you don't have
the numbers.

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:05.870
Now, the argument would be in order to be
thorough here, the argument would be if there

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:11.490
are already four to six Republicans and we
haven't even had a trial, there could be more

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:12.490
coming.

00:03:12.490 --> 00:03:13.799
There could be momentum.

00:03:13.799 --> 00:03:19.739
The best path to making removal of Trump or
reality is something that's not going to happen,

00:03:19.739 --> 00:03:25.110
which is if the Senate passes a measure to
make, make impeachment votes a secret ballot

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:28.230
where they vote privately instead of publicly.

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:32.220
This way you could have exactly 20 Republicans
vote to impeach.

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:38.780
It would get Trump removed, but no one would
initially know which were the 20 that voted

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:39.780
to convict.

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:46.109
But again, there is no indication that there
exists a political appetite for this and the

00:03:46.109 --> 00:03:47.109
Republican party.

00:03:47.109 --> 00:03:51.719
And the question also is, could such a measure
even be passed with Mitch McConnell running

00:03:51.719 --> 00:03:56.879
the show in the Senate as majority leader,
given that he says the white house will be

00:03:56.879 --> 00:04:00.359
in total coordination with him as to how the
Senate trial would be run.

00:04:00.359 --> 00:04:04.319
Certainly the white house would know that
a secret ballot would not be advantageous

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:09.250
to Donald Trump because it would allow some
Republicans, uh, to vote to convict without

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:13.609
suffering the political consequences and the
wrath of Trump as a result.

00:04:13.609 --> 00:04:18.109
Very difficult to imagine that Mitch McConnell
even lets that be a serious discussion.

00:04:18.109 --> 00:04:20.000
That's what we know so far.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:21.000
Tell me what you think.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.000
Agree, disagree.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:26.470
If you're watching on YouTube, leave a comment
of course after hitting, like and subscribe

